ly the frame changed.\nThis suggests:\n- Clicking frame's red: toggles frame color (N\u2194W)\n- Clicking shape's red: toggles both shape (O\u2194W) and frame (N\u2194W)\nCurrent state: shape=W, frame=N\nNow, what's the goal? I've been trying various things without success. Let me think about what combination might solve the level.\nPossible goals:\n1. Both same color: (W, W) or (O, N) - neither achieved yet\n2. Shape=W, frame=W: need to click frame's red (toggles frame N\u2192W) while shape stays W\n3. Shape=O, frame=N: need to click shape's red (toggles both)\nLet me try getting (W, W):\nCurrent: shape=W, frame=N\nClick frame's red: frame N\u2192W, shape stays W \u2192 (W, W)!\nLet me try this!", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# H7 | Clicking frame's red toggles only frame (N\u2194W); clicking shape's red toggles both\n# Current: shape=W, frame=N\n# To get (W, W): click frame's red \u2192 frame N\u2192W, shape stays W\n# Let me try this combination!\n\naction([{'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': 36, 'col': 39}])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation['nodes']\nprint(\"After MOUSE on frame's red:\")\nfor n in seg:\n    if n['pixels'] in [135, 144]:\n        b = n['boundary']\n        rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n        cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n        print(f\"  {n['color']}: rows {min(rows)}-{max(rows)}, cols {min(cols)}-{max(cols)}, pixels={n['pixels']}\")\nprint(f\"  level_completed={last_action_result.get('level_completed')}\")\nprint(f\"  done={last_action_result.get('done')}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "After MOUSE on frame's red:\n  O: rows 11-25, cols 14-28, pixels=135\n  W: rows 29-49, cols 41-49, pixels=144\n  level_completed=False\n  done=False", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "conte